+Unity in the studio (2026-09-11) is verified without a Unity. No editor and no CLI are installed on
+the machine that runs `npm test`, and none may be. Three fakes stand in: `tests/helpers/fake-unity-cli.ts`
+is a shell script that answers the handful of commands the studio speaks (`--version`, `editors
+--installed --json`, `auth status --json`, `license --json`, the ndjson sign-in and editor install,
+`pipeline install`) and logs every argv; `fake-pipeline.ts` is a real `node:http` Pipeline server
+on loopback with bearer auth, the 503 settle gate, the descriptor file, a runtime server that
+appears on `editor_play`, a dialog and a frozen heartbeat on demand, and scripted answers for every
+command the port and the tools send; `fake-unity-editor.ts` is a process the studio spawns exactly
+as it would `Unity.app/Contents/MacOS/Unity` — it writes the descriptor with its own pid, heartbeats
+it, logs every invocation and exec under `Library/`, deletes the descriptor on SIGTERM, and scripts
+its night from files in the project (`Assets/.fake-compile-errors`, `.fake-play-throws`,
+`.fake-crash`, `.fake-settle-ms`). The suites, all in `npm test`: `unity.test.ts` (detection and
+the pin, every status code, the setting, the CLI install against a fake CDN and script, sign-in and
+the editor install, the Pipeline write), `unity-editor.test.ts` (spawn, descriptor, settle, play,
+release, the 503 and 401 paths, one restart, a foreign descriptor refused; the pool's leases, cap,
+queue and version refusal), `unity-gate.test.ts` (the deny list, the entry owner's two commands,
+`save_all` under a lease, paths and confirmation), `unity-ownership.test.ts` (the `.meta` shadow,
+the entry-owned set, both bodies of the rule on one table, the refusal's wording, the locks, a
+fork's cloned `Library/`, the reviewer's arm), `unity-project.test.ts` (the shape, the sheet, the
+once-asked answer, adoption's exact writes, `validateAt`, the grey-box fixture), `unity-template.test.ts`
+(the template's tree, pins, attributes, sizes, GUIDs, the contract page, a scaffold),
+`unity-tools.test.ts` (every tool round-trips through the fake Pipeline on both transports, the
+lease going back in the `finally`, and the `unity:` handle in the RPC surface), `director-unity.test.ts`
+(three rig nights through the real core and harness child: prove a build, judge a still and land
+without touching the browser pool; three loop workers on one queue slot with no two editors
+overlapping and a wait recorded as a hold; a fork that does not compile refused by name with no
+judge call), the `T-Unity` cases in `harness-incidents.test.ts` (the planner's line, the builder's
+block per engine, the grant, and a night with Unity off carrying no Unity word), the Unity cases
+in `engine-voice.test.ts`, `check-grammar.test.ts` (the grammar without `scene` on a Unity night,
+and the seed shipping exactly three skills — `director.md`, `facet-decomposition.md`, `unity.md`)
+and `verdict.test.ts` (`validate` as the fifth `recordVerdict`). `flushUnityEvents()` is what a
+test awaits before reading `unity_editor` events. The two suites that spawn editors themselves
+(`unity-editor`, `unity-tools`) end with `pgrep -f <project root>` in their `after` and assert
+nothing survives; the rig nights rely on `stop()` releasing every editor, and `unityProcessesAlive`
+is the same question asked of the core. `tests/fixtures/unity/grey-box` is the template as a
+brought project, held byte for byte against `src/game-template-unity` where the two overlap and
+carrying none of the studio's pages. The shapes e2e passes `unity: { probe: async () => null }` so
+a developer's local Unity never adds tools mid-comparison; the smoke and self-test apps and every
+rig detect in isolation (`unity: { isolated: true }` — only their own folder and the `pathEnv` or
+`home` a test names, never this Mac's PATH, home or Unity Hub, and no licence file), so the Electron
+e2e smoke's check that the Unity page renders its three rows in the not-set-up state without
+spawning an editor holds on a Mac with Unity installed too — as does its check that a new chat's
+composer carries the Unity switch off and unpressable. The front door itself is
+`unity-front-door.test.ts`: on a rig whose fake CLI names a fake editor, "make me a marsh in
+Unity" and the composer's switch both make the Unity template (stamped with the installed editor,
+bound to the chat, the coordinator having heard `UNITY: available`), a timed build's launch does the
+same and the night validates in that editor, and without Unity the tool refuses, the chat is told,
+and a night's launch is refused with the same words; on a three.js folder the coordinator hears
+"UNITY: not here" with the editor that waits on this Mac (`director.test.ts` asserts the same line
+in a Three director's brief). What
+none of this certifies is a real Unity, which the opt-in runner `npm run test:unity:e2e`
+(`tests/e2e/run-unity-e2e.mjs` → `unity-electron.ts`, not part of `npm run verify`) does: it
+detects Unity first with the real, keychain-free probe and, when the CLI or a Unity 6 editor is
+absent, writes `summary.json { skipped: true, reason }` under `.claude/plans/unity/evidence`
+(gitignored) and exits 0. With Unity present it adopts each fixture under
+`tests/fixtures/unity/<id>/manifest.json` as the user's own project (the copy stamped with the
+installed editor's version), leases a live editor, runs the harness's own `gatherEvidence` with
+`surface: "unity"` twice on one seed and records the shapes runner's rows plus `compiles`,
+`plays`, `stopped-clean`, `queue-serial`, `no-unity-left` and the two transports' tool rows;
+`capture-4k` and `headless-no-window` run since `unityHeadless` is on by default (2026-09-11
+evening): every editor process of ours carries `-batchmode`, and a 4K still from the `default`
+camera in Play mode has lit pixels. On the owner's Mac on 2026-09-11 (Unity 6000.6.0f1, Pipeline
+0.6.0-exp.1, CLI 1.0.0-beta.8) it passed every row — fixture 45/45, Codex 6/6, Claude 8/8 —
+after the wire shapes it found were put into the code (the research's §5 has the measurements);
+the same evening it refused a manifest module that is not in this editor (`com.unity.modules.vr`),
+which is why the template's module list is exactly the installed editor's. The real proof of a night remains one the
+owner watches from the studio's own window.
