@@ -0,0 +1,43 @@
+# Unity workspace — read this page, then `.studio/BRIEF.md`
+
+You are building a game inside the AI Game Studio as a contractor, in a Unity 6 project the studio
+opens in an editor it runs itself. This project starts as a grey box: a ground, four walls, a
+player capsule you walk with WASD and turn with the mouse (`Assets/Player/PlayerController.cs`),
+an overview camera, an eye camera at the player's head, and the studio's probes
+(`Assets/Studio/StudioProbes.cs`). Build the scene and mechanics from the brief; nothing here is
+precious except what the rules below keep. Five rules; tables in `docs/CONTRACT.md`.
+
+1. **Read `.studio/BRIEF.md` first when it exists.** This iteration's contract: the checks the
+   harness verifies, the scoreboard, the attempts that lost, the distance to the reference stills,
+   the recipes that apply. Work identity checks first.
+2. **Work inside your seam.** A seam is a folder under `Assets/` the plan names (`Assets/Lighting/**`,
+   `Assets/Studio/Probes.Lighting.cs`): your scripts, prefabs and materials go there and nowhere
+   else, and a `.meta` belongs to whoever owns the file beside it. The entry owner alone edits
+   `Assets/Scenes/Main.unity`, `ProjectSettings/**` and `Packages/manifest.json`.
+3. **Wire by code, never by hand in the scene.** New things enter the scene through
+   `StudioSpawn.Register("<area>", root => …)` from a `[RuntimeInitializeOnLoadMethod]` in your own
+   file, or through the `SPAWN WIRING` block in `Assets/Studio/StudioSpawn.cs` when you own it.
+   Never hand-edit `.unity` or `.prefab` YAML while an editor holds the project: the editor writes
+   those files on save and your edit is either lost or a corrupt scene. Scene and prefab changes go
+   through the `unity_command` tool, which the editor applies and saves.
+4. **Keep the studio able to see this game.** `StudioProbes` registers `player.x/y/z/yaw`,
+   `actions.primary`, the demo `walk` and the camera `eye:here` with the bridge
+   (`Packages/com.aigamestudio.bridge`) — never remove a registration. Tag everything you add with a
+   `StudioTag` (a group's tag covers what it spawns), register a probe per mechanic, a camera that
+   shows it, a demo when the generic walk cannot reach it. What cannot be measured does not exist.
+   **A HUD is a uGUI Canvas in Screen Space – Camera on the main camera** (uGUI is in the
+   manifest): the studio's stills are camera renders and its editors run with no window, so
+   `OnGUI` and a Screen Space – Overlay canvas are invisible to the judge and to the user's stage.
+5. **Compile, play, look — then finish.** `unity_recompile` first and fix every `error CS` line:
+   a project that does not compile ends the pass with no judge call. Then `unity_play`,
+   `unity_capture` and `unity_state` to see what you built; `unity_console` for the errors the
+   player would see. Randomness only from `Random` after `studio_seed`, time only from
+   `Time.deltaTime`; two builds that cannot be run on one seed cannot be compared. `Library/`,
+   `Temp/`, `Logs/` and `obj/` are the editor's — never edit, commit or lock them.
+
+Two lessons every contractor re-learned: before re-lighting a scene or re-tuning a material,
+capture the game and look at what it already draws; and a check that cannot pass as written is
+not yours to force — write a `HARNESS:` line naming the check id and why in your notes
+(`docs/notes/NOTES.<facet-id>.md` in a facet run, `NOTES.md` in a chat build), whose
+`## Fixed by looking` section is mined into the next run. Helper scripts of your own go under
+`.studio/`, never into `Assets/`.
