+# About this template
+
+The studio's Unity starting point: a Unity 6 URP grey box small enough to commit, written by
+hand (Unity YAML and C#) and checked by `tests/conformance/unity-template.test.ts` for its tree,
+its manifest, its attributes, its sizes and its `.meta` files. This repository's tests never open
+it in an editor (none is installed where they run); it was opened, compiled and played on Unity
+6000.6.0f1 with URP 17.6.0 on the owner's Mac on 2026-09-11 (the research's §5), where the
+opt-in Unity runner (plan §8.4) leases it, plays it and photographs both cameras. The assets Unity
+wrote on that first open are shipped here so a fresh open leaves the tree clean: URP's global
+settings and default volume profile, the rewritten Graphics, EditorBuild and URP assets, the
+Pipeline runtime config (`enableInBuilds: true`, which is what starts the Play-mode input server)
+and the Input System settings asset (input goes to the game view whether or not a window has
+focus, so a headless editor's simulated keys reach the actions). What was written by hand and
+what a different editor release could disagree with:
+
+- `Assets/Scenes/Main.unity` — hand-written scene YAML with fixed fileIDs and the built-in
+  Cube/Capsule meshes (`guid 0000000000000000e000000000000000`). Materials reference URP's own
+  `Lit.mat` (`guid 31321ba15b8f8eb4c954353edc038b1d`): a wrong guid renders magenta, not a
+  crash. Cameras carry no `UniversalAdditionalCameraData`; URP adds it on first use. The
+  `PlayerController`'s `actions` field points at `Player.inputactions` through the Input System
+  importer's main-object fileID; if that reference does not resolve, the controller builds the
+  same three actions in code.
+- `Assets/Settings/URP-Asset.asset` and `URP-Renderer.asset` — minimal
+  `UniversalRenderPipelineAsset` / `UniversalRendererData` (script guids
+  `bf2edee5c58d82540a51f03df9d42094` / `de640fe3d0db1804a85f9fc8f5cadab6`); the editor fills the
+  fields that are missing with their defaults and reloads the renderer's null shader references.
+  `ProjectSettings/GraphicsSettings.asset` names the URP asset; URP creates its global-settings
+  asset on first load.
+- `ProjectSettings/ProjectSettings.asset` — only `activeInputHandler: 1` (the Input System),
+  the product name and the window size; every other field is Unity's default. Its
+  `serializedVersion` is Unity 6000.0's.
+- `ProjectSettings/ProjectVersion.txt` — `{{UNITY_EDITOR_VERSION}}` until `scaffold` stamps the
+  installed LTS (`UNITY_PIN.editorPreferred` by default). No `m_EditorVersionWithRevision`
+  line; Unity Hub reads `m_EditorVersion`.
+- `Packages/manifest.json` — `com.unity.pipeline` pinned to `UNITY_PIN.pipelineVersion`; the
+  URP, Input System and test-framework versions are the ones that opened on Unity 6000.6.0f1 on
+  2026-09-11 (Input System 1.11.2 fails to compile there — its TreeView API became an error — so
+  the pin is 1.20.0, the release that targets Unity 6000; a core package's pin such as URP is
+  re-resolved by the editor to its own bundled version whatever the manifest says). The bridge is
+  `file:com.aigamestudio.bridge`, which the build step embeds beside the manifest.
+- The Pipeline runtime server (Project Settings → Pipeline → Runtime, `enableInBuilds`) is not
+  configured: its settings asset's format is unknown until an editor writes one. Until then the
+  studio drives Play mode through the editor server; `simulate_key` / `simulate_pointer` are
+  proven on the owner's machine first.
+- Every file and folder under `Assets/` and in the bridge package has a `.meta` whose GUID is
+  `md5("<project-relative path>")` — stable across regenerations, unique per path, and the same
+  GUID the scene uses for `m_Script`. A new script needs a new `.meta` by the same rule.
