+# This Unity project's workspace — read this page, then `.studio/BRIEF.md`
+
+You are building inside the AI Game Studio as a contractor, in a Unity project **the studio did
+not write**. It is on Unity __UNITY_VERSION__ with __RENDER_PIPELINE__; the studio opens
+`__ENTRY_SCENE__` in an editor it runs itself, and the wiring file is `__ENTRY_MAIN__`. Its
+folders, its packages, its scenes, its input and its cameras are decisions somebody made — they
+are the game, not defects to correct. Change what the brief asks for and leave the rest standing.
+
+1. **Read `.studio/BRIEF.md` first when it exists.** This iteration's contract: the checks the
+   harness verifies, the scoreboard, the attempts that lost, the distance to the reference stills,
+   the recipes that apply. Work identity checks first.
+2. **Work inside your seam.** A seam is a folder under `Assets/` the plan names (`Assets/Lighting/**`,
+   `Assets/Studio/Probes.Lighting.cs`): your scripts, prefabs and materials go there and nowhere
+   else, and a `.meta` belongs to whoever owns the file beside it. The entry owner alone edits
+   `__ENTRY_SCENE__`, `ProjectSettings/**` and `Packages/manifest.json`.
+3. **Wire by code, never by hand in the scene.** New things enter the scene through
+   `StudioSpawn.Register("<area>", root => …)` from a `[RuntimeInitializeOnLoadMethod]` in your own
+   file, or through the `SPAWN WIRING` block in `__ENTRY_MAIN__` when you own it. Never hand-edit
+   `.unity` or `.prefab` YAML while an editor holds the project: the editor writes those files on
+   save and your edit is either lost or a corrupt scene. Scene and prefab changes go through the
+   `unity_command` tool, which the editor applies and saves.
+4. **Keep the studio able to see this game.** The bridge package (`Packages/com.aigamestudio.bridge`)
+   ATTACHES rather than installs: with no registration it reports `Camera.main` as `default`, the
+   object tagged `Player` (or the first `CharacterController`) as `player.x/y/z/yaw`, and every
+   enabled Input System action as `actions.<name>`. Where `Assets/Studio/StudioProbes.cs` registers
+   more (`StudioBridge.Register`, `RegisterAction`, `RegisterDemo`, `RegisterCamera`), keep those
+   registrations working — a build the judge cannot read is a loss. Tag what you add with a
+   `StudioTag`, register a probe per mechanic, a camera that shows it, a demo when the generic walk
+   cannot reach it. UI you add is a Canvas in Screen Space – Camera on the main camera: the studio's
+   stills are camera renders from an editor with no window, so `OnGUI` and Screen Space – Overlay
+   never reach the judge or the user's stage.
+5. **Compile, play, look — then finish.** `unity_recompile` before anything else, and fix every
+   `error CS` line it reports: a project that does not compile ends the pass with no judge call.
+   Then `unity_play`, `unity_capture` and `unity_state` to see what you built; `unity_console` for
+   the errors the player would see. Determinism: randomness only from `Random` after
+   `studio_seed`, time only from `Time.deltaTime`; two builds that cannot be run on one seed cannot
+   be compared. `Library/`, `Temp/`, `Logs/` and `obj/` are the editor's — never edit, commit or
+   lock them.
+
+Two lessons every contractor re-learned: before re-lighting a scene or re-tuning a material,
+capture the game and look at what it already draws; and a check that cannot pass as written is
+not yours to force — write a `HARNESS:` line naming the check id and why in your notes
+(`docs/notes/NOTES.<facet-id>.md` in a facet run, `NOTES.md` in a chat build), whose
+`## Fixed by looking` section is mined into the next run. Helper scripts of your own go under
+`.studio/`, never into `Assets/`.
