+
+## A Unity night
+
+- There is one live editor, on your integration worktree, and the user's stage shows it. Every
+  worker gets a transient editor on its own worktree and waits its turn in the queue (the
+  `validationQueue` in `run_status` says how many are free). Every judge, health pass and the
+  close compile and play in an editor; a build that does not compile is looked at no further.
+- `validate target=<id>` before you build on a new fork and before you integrate a worker that
+  says it compiles: compile, play, one still, one queue slot, no judge. `worker_start` refuses a
+  fork that does not compile (`does-not-compile`) or fails its tests (`tests-fail`) — read the
+  errors it names, fix them in your worktree with your own hands, `validate integration`, commit.
+- Keep each worker's first compile small: the queue moves one build at a time, and a worker
+  that waits for an editor is held, not lost. Seams are folders under `Assets/<Area>/`, wired in
+  by StudioSpawn; never two owners of the main scene.
+- The project's tests run at integrate (editor and Play mode) and at the close (editor) when it
+  has a test assembly — minutes each; do not ask for them per round.
+- `show` costs nothing for `integration` (the live editor is already there). `show live` or
+  `show <worker>` relaunches the live editor on that folder — a cold import, minutes — and your
+  next health pass relaunches it back. Never casually.
+- A scene or prefab edit goes through the editor's own commands (`unity_command`), never through
+  a hand-edited `.unity` file; a merge conflict in one is resolved with UnityYAMLMerge, which the
+  project registers, not by picking a side.
+- The landing rule has a third road here: a head that compiled and played in the editor lands
+  even when a browser-style health pass had nothing to say. The morning card then reads "made
+  live, compiled and played, not judged better" — one `judge target=integration against=start`
+  is what lets it say more.
