+---
+name: Unity
+description: How a builder works inside a Unity project the studio holds open — the editor is the surface, the bridge is the contract, compile first, play second, look third, and never a hand-edited scene file.
+---
+
+# Unity, the studio's way
+
+The studio opens a Unity 6 editor on your folder and keeps it open for you. Nothing you write
+is real until it compiles and plays there: there is no page, no browser window and no
+`window.__studio`. The `unity_*` tools are your hands and eyes, and every judge of the night
+looks through the same editor you do.
+
+## The loop
+
+1. **Compile first.** After every edit to a `.cs` file, `unity_recompile`. The errors come
+   back verbatim (`Assets/Area/Thing.cs(12,5): error CS1002: ; expected`); fix every one before
+   anything else. A build that does not compile is looked at no further — no play, no still,
+   no judge — and a fork that does not compile refuses every worker.
+2. **Play second.** `unity_play` enters Play mode and answers with the frame it reached; the
+   harness needs frame 2 and no exception in the first seconds. `unity_console` shows the
+   errors since load, minus the ones the build you forked from already had.
+3. **Look third.** `unity_capture` takes a still of the Game view from a registered camera
+   (`camera=eye:here` for the player's own eyes, `view=scene` for the Scene view). The editor
+   runs with no window, so a still is what the camera renders: a HUD on a Screen Space – Camera
+   canvas is in it, `OnGUI` and Screen Space – Overlay are not (`overlay=screen` only ever
+   answers from a shown editor window, and the studio's editors are hidden). `unity_state` answers the registered probes as JSON with
+   `frame`, `time` and `__render.drawCalls`. Look after every meaningful change, and never
+   finish without a still that shows your change from the primary camera.
+4. **Drive it.** `unity_input` plays the game through its own Input System — `keys=w,a,s,d
+   action=press|down|up hold_ms=800`, or `pointer={x,y,button,action}` in the pixels of your
+   last still. It never touches the mouse on this Mac.
+5. **Back in Edit Mode.** `unity_stop` before you finish, and never leave a scene dirty from
+   Play mode: what Play mode changed is thrown away by the editor, and a scene saved from Play
+   mode is a scene nobody can reproduce.
+
+## The seam
+
+- You own a folder under `Assets/<Area>/` (your brief names it). Everything you add lives
+  there: scripts, prefabs, materials, models, an additive scene of your own if you need one.
+- **Wire in by StudioSpawn, never by editing Main.unity.** `Assets/Studio/StudioSpawn.cs` is
+  the registry the base owns: it spawns your prefab or calls your entry at start, so two
+  workers never edit one scene file and the merge never has to reconcile YAML.
+- **Keep the StudioProbes registrations.** The bridge (`com.aigamestudio.bridge`) registers
+  the probes, cameras and demos the harness reads; a build the harness cannot read is a loss.
+  Add probes for what you build (`StudioProbes.Register("player.x", () => …)`), register a
+  camera for what only your camera can show, and a demo (`RegisterDemo`) for a beat the
+  scripted walk never reaches.
+- **Tag what you add with a StudioTag component.** The checks find objects by tag; an untagged
+  object does not count.
+- The main scene (`Assets/Scenes`), `ProjectSettings/` and `Packages/manifest.json` belong to
+  whoever owns main. If your work needs a change there, say so in your report and leave it.
+
+## The editor's own commands
+
+`unity_command` runs any editor command by name — `command=list` for the catalogue,
+`command=<name> args={…}` (JSON) to run one. Scene and prefab edits go through it while you
+hold the editor: creating a prefab, placing an object in your additive scene, setting a
+component field, saving what you changed. The studio forwards the command after a gate — no
+`eval`, no menu, no build, no package or project-settings change, nothing outside your seam —
+and answers with the editor's own reply.
+
+**Never hand-edit a `.unity`, `.prefab` or `.asset` file.** They are YAML full of `fileID`s
+and GUIDs the editor assigns; one typed by hand breaks the reference it names and the
+project with it. If a change needs a scene file, it goes through the editor.
+
+## Your turn on the editor
+
+One editor validates at a time (the studio's queue). Your tools take a turn on first use and
+give it back when your session ends; `unity_session action=status` says where you are in the
+queue and `action=release` gives it back early. A queue wait is minutes, not a failure — the
+harness holds your build unjudged rather than counting it lost, and judges it when an editor
+is free. Keep your first compile small so the queue moves.
+
+## Tests
+
+`unity_test mode=editor` runs the project's editor tests, `mode=playmode` the Play-mode ones,
+`filter=` narrows them. They take minutes: run them when you touched what they cover, never
+after every edit. The harness runs them at integrate and at the close when the project has a
+test assembly.
+
+## What the harness measures
+
+Every board on a Unity night carries `unity-compiles`, `unity-plays` and
+`unity-no-new-errors`, and `unity-tests-pass` where the project has tests. Beside them the
+same checks as any night: pixel, metric and probe checks over the stills and the state, demo
+checks over what you registered, vision and play checks for what only eyes can tell. There is
+no scene census on Unity yet, so a `scene` check is unmeasured here — expose what a check
+needs through a probe instead.
