+++ b/src/unity-bridge/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,44 @@
+# AI Game Studio Bridge — `com.aigamestudio.bridge`
+
+The studio's contract inside a Unity project: the Unity analogue of the browser template's
+`src/studio.js`. Contract version 1.
+
+The studio runs a Unity editor on the project and drives it through Unity's Pipeline package
+(`com.unity.pipeline`, an HTTP server inside the editor). This package adds the commands that
+package does not have — what the *game* knows — and the registry the game fills them from:
+
+- `Runtime/StudioBridge.cs` — `StudioBridge.Register("player.x", () => …)`, `RegisterAction`,
+  `RegisterDemo`, `RegisterCamera`, `Tag`. **Attach, don't install:** with no registration it
+  still answers — `Camera.main` is `default`, the object tagged `Player` (or the first
+  `CharacterController`) supplies `player.x/y/z/yaw`, and every enabled Input System action is
+  counted as `actions.<name>`.
+- `Runtime/StudioBridgeRunner.cs` — the hidden `MonoBehaviour` the bridge creates on first use:
+  the stepped clock, the exception count, the input counters, the render counters, demos.
+- `Runtime/StudioRuntimeCommands.cs` — `studio_state`, `studio_seed`, `studio_pause`,
+  `studio_step`, `studio_start`, `studio_demo`, `studio_demos` (`RuntimeOnly`: executable on the
+  editor server in Play mode and on the runtime server, not advertised in Edit mode).
+- `Editor/StudioEditorCommands.cs` — `studio_cameras`, `studio_camera`, `studio_ready`,
+  `studio_render_stats`.
+
+Every command is a plain static method under `[CliCommand("name", "description")]` from
+`Unity.Pipeline.Commands` (assembly `Unity.Pipeline`); parameters carry `[CliArg]`. The
+attributes are compiled in only when the Pipeline package is present (`STUDIO_PIPELINE`, a
+version define on the asmdef), so the registry compiles on its own.
+
+The studio embeds this folder under `Packages/com.aigamestudio.bridge/` — into its own template
+at build time, and into a project the user brought when they agree to the once-asked write on
+the Open Game sheet (`installPipelineIntoProject`). `game.upgradeContract` tops it up by version.
+
+Measured on Unity 6000.6.0f1 with Pipeline 0.6.0-exp.1 (2026-09-11, the research's §5):
+- The asmdefs reference `Unity.Pipeline` and `Unity.InputSystem` by name; a project without
+  either package does not compile this assembly (an asmdef has no optional reference), which is
+  why the sheet asks for both packages together. Both assemblies compiled clean on the first open.
+- The editor commands (`studio_cameras`, `studio_camera`, `studio_ready`, `studio_render_stats`)
+  are listed in the editor's catalogue; the `RuntimeOnly` ones answer on the editor server while
+  the game plays, without being listed.
+- The runtime server in Play mode needs `ProjectSettings/Packages/com.unity.pipeline/
+  RuntimePipelineConfig.json` with `enableInBuilds: true` (the template ships it); its descriptor
+  is `<project>/.unity-pipeline-runtime-port`. `simulate_key` moves the player; `simulate_pointer`
+  carries no delta, so mouse-look is `studio_look` here (a delta queued on the mouse device).
+- `studio_step n` returns at once and the runner counts the frames down; `studio_ready.stepping`
+  says how many are left, and the studio's port waits on it.
