+# Unity projects nobody opened in an editor here
+
+`tests/fixtures/games` holds browser games the studio did not write; this folder holds the
+Unity side of the same idea. No Unity editor and no Unity CLI are installed on the machine that
+runs `npm test`, so nothing here is ever compiled or played by these tests. What the tests prove
+is everything the studio decides *before* an editor opens: that the folder is read as a Unity
+project (`detectProjectShape`), what the Open Game sheet says about it, what adoption writes and
+never writes, and that the fixture stays a faithful copy of the studio's own template. The
+Unity runner (`npm run test:unity:e2e`, plan §8.4) opens these folders for real on the owner's
+Mac and is the only place a claim about Unity's behaviour is measured.
+
+## The fixtures
+
+| folder | title | what it is | proves |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| `grey-box` | Grey Box | the studio's own template (`src/game-template-unity`) as a brought project: its `Assets/`, its settings, its manifest with the Pipeline package, the bridge package's `package.json` — and none of the studio's pages | detection with and without a WebGL export beside it, the sheet's facts line, the once-asked write, adoption's exact writes |
+| `kart` | (S7's choice) | downloaded by the owner into this folder, gitignored — a real Unity 6 game the studio never saw | a night that adds one feature to a brought game (§7 M1 acceptance d) |
+
+## The rules every fixture keeps
+
+Asserted by `tests/conformance/unity-project.test.ts`:
+
+1. **No studio pages and no `studio.json`.** `CLAUDE.md`, `NOTES.md`, `docs/CONTRACT.md`,
+   `.gitignore` and `.gitattributes` are what adoption writes; a fixture that carries them is
+   testing nothing about adoption.
+2. **The three signals, and nothing of the browser template.** `ProjectSettings/ProjectVersion.txt`
+   with an `m_EditorVersion` line, an `Assets/` folder, `Packages/manifest.json`. Never an
+   `index.html` at the root (a `Build/` export beside the project is made in a temp copy by the
+   test that needs one).
+3. **A `README.md` whose first paragraph says what the fixture proves**, at least 200 characters —
+   adoption seeds `NOTES.md` from exactly that paragraph.
+4. **A `manifest.json`** (version 1, plan §8.4): `shape` deep-equals what `detectProjectShape`
+   answers; `adopts.addsAtLeast` is a subset of `plannedWrites`, `adopts.neverAdded` a
+   must-not-contain; `unity` names the editor version, the input backend and the render pipeline
+   the runner should find; `game`, `setup`, `cameras`, `delta`, `expectWarnings`,
+   `readyBudgetMs` and `queueSlots` are what the runner asserts.
+5. **`grey-box` is the template, byte for byte, where the two overlap** — every file under
+   `Assets/` and the settings it carries — except `ProjectVersion.txt`, whose placeholder is
+   stamped with `6000.0.50f1`. The bridge is a stub: only its `package.json`, enough for the
+   `bridge` fact; the C# lives once, in `src/unity-bridge`.
+6. **No `Library/`, `Temp/`, `Logs/`, `obj/`, `UserSettings/`** — the editor's folders are never
+   committed, here or in any project the studio opens.
